.he "fgrep (1)                    8/16/26                    fgrep (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
fgrep
- print lines containing any of a set of strings
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
fgrep [ option ] [ strings ] [ file ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Fgrep reads the named files, or the standard input with no file, and
writes every line that contains one of the strings.  The strings are
the first argument, or a list of lines in a file named with -f.  A
string is matched as it stands: none of its characters are special,
which is what makes fgrep faster than grep and the one to use for a
literal word or several of them.
.sp
With more than one file, each printed line is preceded by its file
name.  Exit status is 0 if a line matched, 1 if none did, 2 on an
error.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-e strings	Treat strings as the string list even if it begins with -.

.ti -10
-f file	Take the string list, one string to a line, from file.

.ti -10
-v	Print lines that match none of the strings.

.ti -10
-x	Only a line whose whole text is one of the strings matches.

.ti -10
-c	Print a count of matching lines, not the lines themselves.

.ti -10
-l	Print the name of each file that has a match, once.

.ti -10
-n	Precede each line by its line number in the file.

.ti -10
-b	Precede each line by its block number.

.ti -10
-s	Print nothing, only set the exit status.

.ti -10
-h	Do not print the file name with each line, even when there are several files.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The keyword machine is built in a fixed array, and the port made that
array smaller than v7's to fit a 64k image, so a very long list of
strings - more than a thousand or so - overflows with "wordlist too
large".
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
grep (1)
